Satellites — descriptive history.

Hubs and links hold only keys. All the descriptive data — every attribute, every measure — lives in satellites. And a satellite doesn't just store the current value; it stores every value over time. This is where the vault's history lives.

So what's in a satellite? A satellite hangs off one parent — a hub or a link. Sat customer describes hub customer. It has the customer hash key, a foreign key to the parent hub. It has a load date, which is part of the primary key. It has a hash-diff — a hash of the attributes, for change detection. It has the record source. And then the actual descriptive columns: name, email, segment, city, province, region, country, postal code. Now, the primary key is the pair — customer hash key plus load date. And that composite key is the whole trick: many rows per parent, one per point in time.

That's why history is built in. Because the key includes the load date, tracking a change is just an insert. Ana's city changes from Madrid to Barcelona? You insert a new sat customer row, with today's load date and city Barcelona — and the old Madrid row stays. Nothing is overwritten. The satellite is an SCD-2 timeline, natively — no effective and expiry columns to bolt on, because sequencing by load date gives you the versions for free.

There are two kinds of satellite. On a hub, a satellite holds descriptive context — sat customer, sat product; names, categories, prices — the stuff that becomes dimension attributes. On a link, a satellite holds transaction measures — sat sales-measures on link order-line; quantity, line total — the stuff that becomes fact measures.

And one more refinement: a single hub can carry several satellites. Commonly you split them by source system — one satellite per source, each loaded independently — or by rate of change — fast-changing attributes in one satellite, slow ones in another. Splitting keeps the loads parallel, and it avoids rewriting stable data when only a volatile field moves. So: satellites hold all descriptive attributes and measures, keyed by parent plus load date — so every change is a new row, and full history is automatic. They're native SCD-2, split by source or volatility for efficient parallel loads.
